.TH POMO 1 "October 2026" "pomo 1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
pomo \- simple command line pomodoro timer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pomo
[\fB\-w\fR \fImin\fR] [\fB\-s\fR \fImin\fR] [\fB\-l\fR \fImin\fR] [\fB\-e\fR \fIn\fR] [\fB\-n\fR \fItype\fR] [\fB\-m\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pomo
takes over the terminal and shows a large countdown. A work timer
(25 minutes by default) starts right away. When it ends you are notified and
a short break (5 minutes) is queued but not started. Every fourth pomodoro a
long break (15 minutes) is queued instead. When a break ends, the next
pomodoro is queued the same way. Work is shown in red, breaks in green, and a
paused or queued timer is dimmed.
.PP
Sounds and the bell always play unless muted with
.B m
(or turned off with
.B \-n desktop
or
.BR "\-n none" ).
Desktop notifications and un-minimizing are skipped while the pomo terminal has focus, since you can already see it. Focus is detected with terminal focus reporting; in a terminal without it, pomo assumes it is focused unless started with
.BR \-m .
.SH KEYS
Under the timer, a history lists each finished timer, newest first: the time it ended, its name, how long it actually ran, and whether it was ended early with
.B b
or
.BR w .
.TP
.B space
Pause, resume, or start a queued timer (\fBEnter\fR and \fBp\fR do the same).
.TP
.B 1
Add 1 minute.
.TP
.B 5
Add 5 minutes.
.TP
.B 0
Add 10 minutes.
.TP
.B +
Add any number of minutes: type the number, then Enter. Esc cancels.
.TP
.B s
Set the timer to any number of minutes: type the number, then Enter. Esc cancels.
.TP
.B m
Mute or unmute sounds and the bell. Desktop notifications still appear.
.TP
.B v
Switch between the big digits and a progress bar. The bar hides the time left, so there is nothing to watch tick down.
.TP
.B r
Restart the current timer from its full length.
.TP
.B b
During work: end the pomodoro now (it counts) and start the break.
.TP
.B w
During a break: skip the rest of it and start working.
.TP
.B q
Quit (Ctrl\-C also works).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-w " min"
Work (pomodoro) length in minutes. Default 25.
.TP
.BI \-s " min"
Short break length in minutes. Default 5.
.TP
.BI \-l " min"
Long break length in minutes. Default 15.
.TP
.BI \-e " n"
Take a long break every \fIn\fR pomodoros. Default 4.
.TP
.BI \-n " type"
Notification type: \fBsound\fR, \fBdesktop\fR, \fBboth\fR (default),
\fBbell\fR (terminal bell only) or \fBnone\fR.
.TP
.B \-m
Start minimized. When any timer ends, the window is restored and a desktop
notification is shown that stays until dismissed, whatever \fB\-n\fR is set
to. Clicking a pomo notification brings the window back.
.IP
On GNOME (Wayland) this needs the bundled shell extension; install it with
\fBmake install\-extension\fR, log out and back in, then run
\fBgnome\-extensions enable pomo@ajchurchill\fR. pomo finds its window by the
terminal title \fIpomo [PID]\fR, so the pomo tab must be the visible tab of its
window. Without the extension, xterm window codes are used instead, which
most Wayland terminals ignore.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Settings are read from \fI$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/pomo/config\fR (normally
\fI~/.config/pomo/config\fR) as \fIkey = value\fR lines; \fB#\fR starts a
comment. Command line options override the file. Keys:
.PP
.nf
work = 25
short = 5
long = 15
every = 4
notify = both
minimized = no
sound_work_done  = /usr/share/sounds/freedesktop/stereo/complete.oga
sound_break_done = /usr/share/sounds/freedesktop/stereo/message.oga
sound_long_break = /usr/share/sounds/freedesktop/stereo/bell.oga
.fi
.PP
Each event has its own sound. Any file playable by \fBpw\-play\fR,
\fBpaplay\fR or \fBcanberra\-gtk\-play\fR works; if none is available
the terminal bell is used.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B pomo
Standard 25/5/15 session.
.TP
.B pomo \-w 50 \-s 10 \-n desktop
50 minute pomodoros, 10 minute breaks, desktop notifications only.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR notify\-send (1),
.BR pw\-play (1)
